{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b2930af9d2e7e426", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tComparative Analysis of Spread-Spectrum Codes for Fibre-Optic Distributed Acoustic Sensing Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Submit without GitHub Submit in GitHub arXiv is now an independent nonprofit! Learn more × Back to arXiv Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Abstract I Introduction II Coded sequences II-A Correlation analysis in ideal conditions II-B Time-frequency response II-C Correlation analysis in the presence of laser phase noise III Sequence comparison on a DAS system III-A Received intensities III-B Performance under different laser phase noise conditions IV Conclusions V Acknowledgments References License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2608.06864v1 [eess.SP] 07 Aug 2026 Comparative Analysis of Spread-Spectrum Codes for Fibre-Optic Distributed Acoustic Sensing Maria Freire-Hermelo and Élie Awwad Manuscript created February, 2026. Maria Freire-Hermelo and Élie Awwad are with the COMELEC department, LTCI, Télécom Paris, IP Paris, 19 place Marguerite Perey, Palaiseau, France (e-mail: maria.freirehermelo@telecom-paris.fr, elie.awwad@telecom-paris.fr). Abstract This paper presents a comprehensive performance comparison of several coded sequences for distributed acoustic sensing systems through numerical simulations. Correlation analysis reveals that perfect autocorrelation sequences provide accurate channel estimation. Phase noise sensitivity varies significantly with frequency diversity, with high-diversity sequences maintaining lower estimation errors for phase and intensity despite increased laser linewidths. For a coded distributed acoustic sensing system with \\qty 10 laser linewidth, high-diversity sequences achieve [MATH] rad phase error compared to [MATH] rad for low-diversity codes for a \\qty 1.5 \\kilo fibre length. The findings establish performance trade-offs between correlation quality, phase noise resilience, and practical implementation constraints for next-generation fibre-optic distributed acoustic sensing systems. I Introduction Recently, Distributed Acoustic Sensing (DAS) has emerged as a versatile technology for fibre-optic sensing, enabling continuous infrastructure monitoring and providing timely alerts for different events, such as earthquake detection [ 9 ] . Compared to pulsed laser interrogation techniques, whose measurement sensitivity is limited by high peak power requirements, continuous-wave DAS systems employing coded sequences to modulate the transmitted signal, also known as spread-spectrum DAS , have shown promise in reducing peak power, thereby mitigating nonlinear crosstalk and enabling potential coexistence of fibre communications and sensing on the same fibre [ 3 ] . Such coexistence of data transmission and DAS over a single optical fibre exemplifies optical-fiber-based integrated sensing and communication (O-ISAC) , where sensing and communications share the same infrastructure [ 11 ] . To unlock the potential of O-ISAC , optimal coded sequences are essential for reliable DAS performance. Yet determining the best choice for transmission in DAS systems remains an open research problem. Previous studies have investigated the use of Legendre, Maximum Length Sequence (m-sequence) and Golay codes [ 14 , 15 , 7 ] ; however, comprehensive comparative analyses across different sequence types are still limited. To date, only one study has numerically compared the performance of Golay complementary sequences with that of Perfect-Square Minimum-Phase (PS-MP) Constant Amplitude Zero Auto-Correlation (CAZAC) sequences [ 6 ] . Here, we present a detailed evaluation of various coded sequences applied within a DAS system, in particular within the coherent [MATH] -OTDR implementation of DAS, focusing particularly on laser phase noise, one of the main limitations of such systems [ 18 , 2 ] . Section II introduces the selected sequences and discusses their key properties. Section III analyses their performance using a simulation framework under different laser phase noise conditions. Numerical analysis confirms the impact of laser phase noise on performance for all sequences, revealing differences that depend on the frequency diversity characteristics of each sequence. For a \\qty 2.55 \\kilo fibre length, more frequency-diverse sequences achieve lower phase errors of [MATH] rad compared to [MATH] rad for less frequency-diverse sequences. II Coded sequences The following coded sequences, commonly employed in previous DAS studies, are considered in this work to compare their performance under various laser phase noise conditions [ 14 , 15 , 7 ] . In all definitions below, [MATH] denotes the sequence length, whose exact value depends on the type of sequence considered: • m-sequence : A maximum length Pseudorandom Binary Sequence (PRBS) generated by a Linear-Feedback Shift Register (LFSR) , used in several DAS studies [ 14 , 15 ] . Encoded in the set [MATH] , with sequence length [MATH] for a register length [MATH] , achieving the maximum possible period. No padding or truncation is considered, as it would degrade autocorrelation properties. • Legendre: Also applied in DAS experiments [ 15 ] , a Legendre sequence of length [MATH] , where [MATH] is an odd prime of the form [MATH] ( [MATH] being an integer) is determined as [ 19 , 17 ] : [MATH] (1) • Golay complementary sequences: Adopted in DAS systems, these real-valued sequences can also be defined over the alphabet [MATH] . Pairs of mutually orthogonal Golay sequences exist and have been exploited for dual-polarisation probing [ 7 ] . Golay complementary sequences can be constructed recursively from smaller Golay sequences. For length [MATH] where [MATH] is the recursion index (starting from base sequences of length 4), we denote [MATH] and [MATH] as two such orthogonal pairs, constructed recursively as [ 7 ] : [MATH] (2) In transmission, the pair [MATH] is sent sequentially over one polarization, while [MATH] is sent over the orthogonal polarization, enabling polarization-multiplexed Golay probing [ 7 ] . • PS-MP CAZAC : These sequences have been previously applied for channel estimation in optical communication systems and proposed for DAS applications [ 7 , 16 ] . For a PS-MP CAZAC sequence of length [MATH] , where [MATH] with [MATH] being a non-zero positive integer, each symbol [MATH] is defined as: [MATH] (3) where [MATH] ranges from 0 to [MATH] . The resulting complex symbols have constant amplitude and uniformly distributed phase over the unit circle. • Zadoff-Chu (ZC) CAZAC : Another type of CAZAC sequence, the ZC sequence provides complex symbols of constant amplitude with phases over the unit circle. Each symbol [MATH] , for [MATH] ranging from 0 to [MATH] , is defined as: [MATH] (4) where [MATH] is called the root index, generating a distinct ZC sequence (chosen coprime with [MATH] for optimal correlation properties) [ 4 , 1 ] . Modifying [MATH] adjusts the sequence frequency diversity, i.e. how broadly the sequence energy spreads across frequencies. The following analysis uses three ZC sequences with distinct [MATH] values yielding minimum ( [MATH] ), medium ( [MATH] ), and maximum ( [MATH] ) frequency diversity, as shown in the spectrograms in Section II-B . CAZAC sequences, including PS-MP and ZC types, generate complex-valued symbols on the unit circle. While PS-MP corresponds to a [MATH] - Phase Shift Keying (PSK) constellation (where [MATH] defines both sequence length [MATH] and constellation size), ZC sequences present more challenging optical generation using an electro-optic modulator due to their non-uniform phase distribution. The remaining sequences ( m-sequence , Legendre, Golay) employ real-valued Binary Phase-Shift Keying (BPSK) modulation over the alphabet [MATH] . For m-sequences and Legendre sequences, a non-binary mapping [MATH] with